WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.679 --> 00:00:05.009
Dozens of hopeful TV show creators head to
the big networks every year, looking to get

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:06.009
their show on the air.

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:10.170
Very few make it to the pilot stage and even
less actually make it to series.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.200
However, even with such a long and competitive
process, not all of the shows that hit the

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.510
air are instant hits.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:20.100
Some, in fact, are so badly received by audiences,
they're axed before they even get off the

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.100
ground.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.630
Here are some TV shows that were canceled
before they hit the five-episode mark.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:26.810
Anchorwoman

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:30.850
It only took one night for Fox to decide they
didn't want to see any more of their reality

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:32.189
show Anchorwoman.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:37.990
"In 15 days, her dream comes true.

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:39.649
Lauren Jones at 5.

00:00:39.649 --> 00:00:40.649
She's coming."

00:00:40.649 --> 00:00:45.280
In August of 2007, the show was canceled after
airing two back-to-back episodes, reaching

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.780
only 2.7 million viewers.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.670
The would-be series followed former The Price
Is Right model Lauren Jones as she tried to

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.420
become a news anchor at a Texas TV station.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:59.200
The series pitted Jones against the station's
5 p.m. anchor, Annalisa Petralia, and Fox

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.720
even promoted their rivalry on their website.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.790
But needless to say, the show was trashed
by critics once it premiered.

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:06.110
The LA Times concluded,

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:11.530
"If you hate women, men, Texas, Los Angeles,
television news and any of the social progress

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:16.210
made by Americans in the 20th century, then
Anchorwoman is the show for you."

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:17.990
The Beautiful Life: TBL

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:22.400
Mischa Barton's dramatic exit from The O.C.
may have left fans reeling, but they still

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.490
weren't ready to follow Marissa Cooper to
her next project.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:31.080
Barton's 2009 CW series The Beautiful Life:
TBL, only lasted for two episodes before it

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.159
was canceled due to low ratings.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:37.240
Even though it featured a beautiful star-studded
cast, and was executive produced by Ashton

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:42.270
Kutcher, it still only brought in just over
1 million viewers for its second episode.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.200
Fans were upset to see the show canceled,
and more than 8,000 people signed a petition

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.299
to bring it back, to no avail.

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:52.090
Kutcher was adamant about still getting the
produced episodes of the show out to the public

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:55.830
when it went off the air, landing a deal with
Hewlett-Packard to have them all uploaded

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.020
to YouTube for fans to watch, ad free.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.020
Coupling

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:05.150
The Office may be the model example of a British
TV series turning into a successful American

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.689
sitcom, but many attempts to take comedies
stateside have failed.

00:02:08.689 --> 00:02:14.269
One such experiment was Coupling, NBC's 2003
attempt at adapting the BBC Two romantic comedy

00:02:14.269 --> 00:02:16.409
series from Doctor Who's Steven Moffat.

00:02:16.409 --> 00:02:20.099
The show is based on his relationship with
producer Sue Vertue.

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:23.430
NBC started looking into the show when Friends
was nearing the end of its run.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.970
However, their attempts to bring Coupling
to the States were troubled from the start,

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.400
with the network shooting and then scrapping
a pilot because they felt Moffat's script

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.790
was too confusing.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:35.090
Three of the six lead roles were recast when
the pilot was re-shot.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:38.939
"You want us to split up?"

00:02:38.939 --> 00:02:39.939
"Yes."

00:02:39.939 --> 00:02:41.510
"I don't accept."

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:46.309
Coupling ended up getting the axe after only
four low-rated episodes on NBC, although Moffat

00:02:46.309 --> 00:02:50.269
had much more success with the British version,
which ran for four series.

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:53.249
Moffat blamed NBC for the show's cancellation
and later said,

00:02:53.249 --> 00:02:55.109
"I learned not to work in America.

00:02:55.109 --> 00:02:59.530
I say that slightly facetiously, but truthfully,
I'm British, what is the point of me going

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.980
to try to make it somewhere else?"

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.669
Ironside

00:03:03.669 --> 00:03:07.319
Remakes may be all the rage nowadays, but
not all of them are successful.

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:12.380
Such was the case with NBC's 2013 attempt
at remaking the popular '60s and '70s cop

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:16.849
procedural Ironside, this time with Blair
Underwood starring as the title character,

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:20.419
a wheelchair-bound police officer who solves
the city's most difficult cases.

00:03:20.419 --> 00:03:22.500
"How the hell'd you see that?"

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.120
"I got a different view of the world from
down here."

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.159
Underwood's take on the show was imagined
as grittier and darker than the original,

00:03:29.159 --> 00:03:31.010
which didn't sit well with viewers.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:35.310
The show was also hit with controversy from
disability rights advocates, with some groups

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:40.079
calling out NBC for casting a non-disabled
actor in the role of a disabled character.

00:03:40.079 --> 00:03:44.639
(The show's producers claimed they needed
an able-bodied actor for flashback sequences,

00:03:44.639 --> 00:03:45.639
pre-wheelchair.)

00:03:45.639 --> 00:03:49.739
The show was trashed by critics, airing only
three episodes before it was pulled from the

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:50.960
schedule.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:52.220
Made in Jersey

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:57.220
Being shuffled to Friday nights is never a
good sign, and the 2012 CBS procedural Made

00:03:57.229 --> 00:04:00.510
in Jersey was sentenced to the fatal slot
before it even started.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:04.420
The legal drama starred Janet Montgomery as
a New Jersey born-and-raised attorney who

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.700
gets a job at a big New York City law firm.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:08.310
"This isn't my first murder case.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.120
I did major crimes in Trenton."

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.120
"Trenton.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.419
I bet you're amazing with a jury.

00:04:12.419 --> 00:04:13.900
They must totally relate to you."

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.979
The show received mixed reviews from critics,
but didn't fare well in the ratings.

00:04:17.979 --> 00:04:23.270
After premiering to 7.8 million viewers, its
second episode dropped to 5.8 million.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:28.849
CBS ended up pulling the show and replacing
it with Undercover Boss after just two episodes.

00:04:28.849 --> 00:04:30.590
The Paul Reiser Show

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:34.449
Launching a show with a big name comedian
has worked well in the past, but that wasn't

00:04:34.449 --> 00:04:36.800
the case for NBC's The Paul Reiser Show.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.789
The 2011 series featured the Mad About You
actor as a former TV star who decides to find

00:04:41.789 --> 00:04:45.360
his next big project after taking a few years
off to be with his family.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.430
"I'm exactly famous enough so it's never good.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:49.430
Only bad."

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:53.629
Although Reiser holds four Golden Globe nominations
for his work on Mad About You, critics were

00:04:53.629 --> 00:04:57.639
less kind when it came to his own sitcom,
slamming it in part for feeling like a less

00:04:57.639 --> 00:04:59.960
fun copy of Curb Your Enthusiasm.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:05.639
The show premiered to only 3.3 million viewers
and its second episode dropped to 2.5 million.

00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:08.340
NBC pulled it and replaced it with reruns
of The Office.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.889
In a prickly appearance on The Tonight Show,
Reiser joked,

00:05:10.889 --> 00:05:14.680
"It turns out they said we enjoy missing you
more than actually having you."

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.129
The Playboy Club

00:05:16.129 --> 00:05:21.539
Following the lives of 1960s playboy bunnies
may seem like a great idea for a TV show,

00:05:21.539 --> 00:05:24.710
but NBC's The Playboy Club proved it definitely
wasn't.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:29.910
The 2011 historical drama featured Eddie Cibrian
as an attorney with political aspirations

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.290
who frequented the notorious Playboy Club.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:36.340
The Club's bunnies featured future stars Amber
Heard, Jenna Dewan, and Laura Benanti.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.340
"I'm gonna be the first chocolate centerfold.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:39.340
I AM!

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:41.740
You can't discriminate against these babies."

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:45.540
The Playboy Club felt some backlash, with
the Parents Television Council calling the

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:49.320
show "degrading and sexualizing" and asking
audiences to boycott.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:53.789
Their request was backed up by activist Gloria
Steinem, who said the show portrayed the "tackiest

00:05:53.789 --> 00:05:55.630
place on earth" as "glamorous."

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:59.740
Although many blamed the behind-the-scenes
drama for the show's bad buzz, some insiders

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:03.259
offered other reasons for the show's failure
to connect with audiences.

00:06:03.259 --> 00:06:04.259
One source said,

00:06:04.259 --> 00:06:05.749
"It was a fundamentally flawed concept.

00:06:05.749 --> 00:06:08.500
It's a soap, which inherently appeals to women.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.300
But it's a brand so tied to men.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.999
I just think it was doomed from the start."

00:06:11.999 --> 00:06:16.900
The series brought in only five million viewers
in its premiere, falling to 3.8 million and

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:20.379
3.2 million for its final two episodes.

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:21.379
We Are Men

00:06:21.379 --> 00:06:26.250
The quick demise of CBS' We Are Men proves
that sometimes even big stars aren't enough

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:27.250
to save a show.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:31.409
The comedy series brought together Tony Shalhoub,
Kal Penn, and Jerry O'Connell, along with

00:06:31.409 --> 00:06:35.080
Rob Greenberg, but their collective star power
couldn't prevent the show from getting the

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:36.110
axe after two episodes.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:39.080
"Dad you know Fiona's mom would totally go
out with you."

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:40.430
"I'll ask her to lunch."

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:41.430
"Great!

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:42.590
She's super nice and has really big boobs."

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:43.720
"Or dinner."

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:48.669
We Are Men premiered to 6.4 million viewers,
dropping to 5.4 million viewers for its second

00:06:48.669 --> 00:06:49.669
episode.

00:06:49.669 --> 00:06:53.151
The show failed to hold onto the audience
of its lead in, How I Met Your Mother, and

00:06:53.151 --> 00:06:58.629
its performance made it a disappointing precursor
to CBS' other big comedy 2 Broke Girls.

00:06:58.629 --> 00:07:02.099
The network determined that reruns of The
Big Bang Theory — and, eventually, Mike

00:07:02.099 --> 00:07:04.919
and Molly — would fare better in the slot.

00:07:04.919 --> 00:07:06.249
Wicked City

00:07:06.249 --> 00:07:11.479
The Ed Westwick-led drama, Wicked City, was
set in 1982 Los Angeles and followed two police

00:07:11.479 --> 00:07:14.870
detectives on a mission to capture the notorious
Hollywood Slayer.

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:16.330
And it lasted only three episodes.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:18.560
"What are you feeling?"

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:19.560
"Terrified."

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:21.960
"There's no turning back now."

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.219
Wicked City was canceled midway through production
on its eighth episode, but the show's producers

00:07:26.219 --> 00:07:30.550
were allowed to finish it up so that it could
become the show's "finale," for online streaming.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:34.199
Executive producer Laurie Zacks assured fans
at the time that the final episode made the

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.440
series' story "complete."

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:37.480
Work It

00:07:37.949 --> 00:07:42.139
Some shows seem like they are doomed right
from the premise, and Work It may be a prime

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:43.139
example.

00:07:43.139 --> 00:07:48.240
The ill-advised ABC sitcom starred Ben Koldyke
and Amaury Nolasco as two recently laid-off

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.849
men who decide to remedy their situation by
posing as women to get jobs at a pharmaceutical

00:07:52.849 --> 00:07:53.360
company.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:54.220
"Excuse me."

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:55.280
"Oh my God."

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:56.500
"Help me.

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:58.080
Please."

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:03.480
The show was, predictably, met with controversy
from many LGBTQ groups, including GLAAD, who

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:06.740
said that the show was harmful to the transgender
community.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:11.360
GLAAD's then-Acting President Mike Thompson
said that the show would contribute to transphobia

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:15.560
and the idea that trans people are "men pretending
to be women" rather than people living their

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:16.740
lives authentically.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:21.009
The show earned even more criticism when the
pilot aired, with Puerto Rican activists taking

00:08:21.009 --> 00:08:25.099
objection to Nolasco's character saying in
the pilot that, because he is Puerto Rican,

00:08:25.099 --> 00:08:26.820
he would be good at selling drugs.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.979
But, controversy aside, Work It just wasn't
a good show.

00:08:29.979 --> 00:08:35.289
The series was absolutely trashed by critics,
and sat at a five percent on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:38.930
After two episodes, it was sent to "work it"
wherever TV shows go when they die.

00:08:38.930 --> 00:08:39.930
Thanks for watching!

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:42.300
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:44.510
Plus, check out this other cool stuff we know
you'll love too!

